<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Guo</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 655 BCE (approx.) · capital Shangyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ji-clan state that moved from the Wei valley to the Yellow River, leaving the rich Guo cemetery at Sanmenxia, and fell to Jin in the famous ruse of the borrowed road.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-state">Guo</a> was founded by a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley. Around the end of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a>, its rulers moved east to Shangyang, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanmenxia">Sanmenxia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, controlling a key crossing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-state">Guo</a> were senior ministers at the Zhou court and led royal campaigns into the 7th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangcunling-cemetery">Shangcunling cemetery</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanmenxia">Sanmenxia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> pits and the tomb of Guo Ji containing jade burial garments and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China's</a> earliest iron-bladed swords, shows the state's wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 655 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-jin">Duke Xian of Jin</a>, having bribed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-yu">State of Yu</a> to let his army pass, captured Shangyang and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-state">Guo</a>, then destroyed Yu on the way home. The episode gave Chinese the idiom 'borrowing a road to destroy Guo'.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-guo.jpg" alt="Western Guo" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 655 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shangyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duke Chou of Guo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guo (Shangyang)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-850&amp;focus=western-guo" aria-label="Western Guo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7675 1379 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Guo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7995 1635h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7995 1635h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shangyang. <a href="/atlas?year=-850&amp;focus=western-guo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

